    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Well, we'll point to other good aspects of the bill. I mean, for example, we are expanding the use of HSAs, which is a good thing. That's more consumer-driven competition in the marketplace. Of course, are that. we will allow people to purchase health care plans using HSAs. Now, that's actually a pretty big deal, Vicki. You will see a lot more money being plotted into HSAs because now you are actually equalizing the treatment for those individuals on the individual market. I mean, think about it. Those individuals, the 5% of American population that have seen their premiums double and triple, have to purchase those insurance policies with after-tax dollars, unlike the rest of us who get group policy that Our insurance is first to the pre-tax dollars. We are turning the management of Medicaid over to the states, which is also a good thing. And I was in Wisconsin last week. I met with a number of people. I think I was made a pretty effective case to calm individuals down. They were concerned their Medicaid is going to be taken away from them. Nothing could be further from the truth in Wisconsin because we are not an expansion state. What we're ramping back here on Medicaid is Medicaid expansion, which covers able-bodied, working-age, childless adults. At a higher percentage than what federal government's funding disabled children, for example. So I'm trying to protect Medicaid for disabled individuals, disabled children, children, and the elderly population, because what's being put at risk is that legacy Medicaid, because the Medicaid expansion where the federal government's paying more than 90% to fund Medicaid for able-bodied childless working-age adults. And by the way, when I took that message into Wisconsin, I had some disabled individuals agree with me. I mean, they came in the meeting, concerned, you're trying to convince me, don't do this. And I think they locked the meeting going, we shouldn't be giving Medicaid dollars to able-bodied working-age childless adults. And that's kind of our point.",
